<div class="wrap">
  <header class="page-head">
    <h1>Features</h1>
    <p class="lead">What Ferrite does on the RT-950 Pro today. Every screen here is the firmware's own, drawn from
      the same code that runs on the radio.</p>
  </header>

  <section class="feature">
    <div class="prose">
      <p class="kicker">Everyday</p>
      <h2>Three slots, and watch</h2>
      <p>The radio has three slots, A, B and C. Each one is on a VFO or a channel. The active slot is the big one;
        the other two are cards, and the radio keeps listening to them too.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Watch</b> listens to two or three slots. The active slot is heard first; the radio never changes it
          by itself.</li>
        <li><b>PTT A</b> transmits on the active slot, <b>PTT B</b> always on slot B.</li>
        <li><b>Mute or turn off</b> a busy slot with <kbd>*</kbd>, without losing it.</li>
        <li><b>Scan</b> a zone or a band while you listen on another slot.</li>
        <li><b>Channels in zones</b>, like folders: "Repeaters", "Air", the weather channels. A Nearby list with a
          GPS fix.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="shots"><figure class="screen"><img src="/img/home.webp" alt="The home screen: slot A on a VFO, B on a repeater, C scanning airband" width="240" height="320"></figure>{{ shot("02-mute-off", "Slot A muted, slot B turned off") }}</div>
  </section>

  <section class="feature flip">
    <div class="prose">
      <p class="kicker">Keys</p>
      <h2>One key layout, learned once</h2>
      <p>The keys do the same thing on every screen and in every mode. The bar at the bottom always shows what
        they do next.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Quick settings:</b> <kbd>MENU</kbd>, then a digit: squelch, power, mode, tones, offset, step, watch,
          save, Battery Save.</li>
        <li><b>Hold a digit</b> for a shortcut: the band scope, power, the tone picker, shift.</li>
        <li><b>Edit a slot in place:</b> hold <kbd>SLOT</kbd>, and every setting is on one screen.</li>
        <li><b>The full menu</b> in nine groups, with number paths you can learn: <kbd>MENU</kbd> <kbd>0</kbd>
          <kbd>5</kbd> <kbd>2</kbd> is always APRS messages.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="shots">{{ shot("01-quick-settings", "Quick settings for the active slot") }}{{ shot("01-edit-slot", "Editing a slot in place") }}</div>
  </section>

  <section class="feature">
    <div class="prose">
      <p class="kicker">Transmit</p>
      <h2>Talk, and repeaters</h2>
      <p>FM voice on your region's amateur bands: 2 m, 70 cm, 6 m, 1.25 m where it is allowed, and 17 m to 10 m.
        You pick the region in setup.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><b>The Transmit screen</b> shows the frequency, power, your mic level and the time left. If the radio
          won't transmit, it says why and which setting fixes it.</li>
        <li><b>Add a repeater</b> in a few presses: the shift fills in from the band plan.</li>
        <li><b>Tones</b> (CTCSS and DCS), a <b>tone search</b> for an unknown one, and the 1750 Hz burst.</li>
        <li><b>Crossband repeater</b> between a VHF and a UHF slot.</li>
        <li>VOX, mic gain, DTMF, CW ID, busy lockout and a transmit timeout.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="shots">{{ shot("03-transmit", "The Transmit screen with mic level and timeout") }}{{ shot("03-crossband", "The crossband repeater page") }}</div>
  </section>

  <section class="feature flip">
    <div class="prose">
      <p class="kicker">APRS</p>
      <h2>APRS, on the radio itself</h2>
      <p>APRS mode gives slot C to APRS and decodes it in the background, while A and B stay free for voice.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Messages</b> with predictive text and quick replies, retried up to 5 times until acknowledged.</li>
        <li><b>Stations heard</b>, with distance, direction and route. Save one as a waypoint.</li>
        <li><b>Beacons</b> by hand, on a timer, or Smart (following your speed), and a list of which digipeaters
          repeated yours.</li>
        <li><b>KISS TNC</b> over the cable or Bluetooth, for apps on your computer or phone.</li>
        <li>FX.25 error correction, digipeating, Mic-E and telemetry.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="shots">{{ shot("05-aprs-card", "The APRS slot on the home screen") }}{{ shot("05-heard", "Stations heard, with distance and direction") }}</div>
  </section>

  <section class="feature">
    <div class="prose">
      <p class="kicker">Listen</p>
      <h2>Broadcast FM, shortwave and weather</h2>
      <p>A third receiver chip handles broadcast FM, AM and shortwave on its own.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><b>FM broadcast on any slot</b>, with stereo and station text (RDS). Your other slots keep watching;
          the music goes quiet when one of them receives.</li>
        <li><b>HF mode</b> for shortwave and the HF amateur bands: AM everywhere, FM from 18 MHz.</li>
        <li><b>The band scope</b> shows activity around your frequency at a glance.</li>
        <li><b>Weather radio</b> for the US and Canada, with SAME alerts for your area.</li>
        <li><b>DTMF digits</b> heard show on the slot's card.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="shots">{{ shot("06-fm", "FM broadcast") }}{{ shot("06-hf", "HF mode on 31 m") }}</div>
  </section>

  <section class="feature flip">
    <div class="prose">
      <p class="kicker">GPS and Bluetooth</p>
      <h2>Where you are, and a link to your computer</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><b>GPS</b>: position, grid square, altitude, speed and a sky plot. On Auto, it runs only while
          something needs your position.</li>
        <li><b>Waypoints</b> with a dial that turns as you move, and an arrival alarm.</li>
        <li><b>The clock</b> sets itself from the GPS.</li>
        <li><b>Bluetooth</b> for Ferrite Console or an APRS app, with an optional PIN.</li>
        <li><b>Ferrite Console</b> in Chrome or Edge: a live view of the screen, channels with CHIRP CSV import,
          settings, APRS messages and a map, listening on the computer, and updates.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="shots">{{ shot("07-gps", "GPS position, grid square and nearest waypoint") }}{{ shot("07-waypoints", "Waypoints, nearest first") }}</div>
  </section>

  <section class="feature">
    <div class="prose">
      <p class="kicker">Power</p>
      <h2>A battery that lasts</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Dynamic clocking:</b> the processor runs at 24 MHz while the radio waits, and at full speed only
          while something needs it. Nothing to set.</li>
        <li><b>Battery Save</b> lets the receivers sleep between short checks, at three depths.</li>
        <li><b>Low Power Mode</b> keeps only what you need to talk on one frequency. On Auto, it comes on by
          itself below 20 %. Tuning, the menus and editing still work.</li>
        <li><b>Night red</b> two presses away, and a high-contrast daylight theme for the sun.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="shots">{{ shot("08-low-power", "Low Power Mode, one slot in an amber frame") }}{{ shot("08-night-red", "The night red theme") }}</div>
  </section>

  <section class="section column-in" id="why">
    <div class="prose">
      <p class="kicker">Why change firmware</p>
      <h2>Why Ferrite instead of stock</h2>
      <p>The RT-950 Pro is good hardware. Ferrite is a different idea of how to use it: one fixed key layout, three
        slots that listen at once, APRS and the decoders on the radio itself, and a lot of attention to battery
        life. Some things it keeps exactly as they were:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Your calibration</b> is used as it is and never erased.</li>
        <li><b>The radio's own bootloader</b> installs every image and is never changed, so an update can always be
          tried again.</li>
        <li><b>Going back</b> is built into Ferrite Console: it writes your old channels and settings back, then
          the original firmware.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The how-to for every feature is in the <a href="{{ site.urls.manual }}">user guide</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

</div>
